exec(open('c3.py').read())
# ---------------- muscular
case('em-compartment','Severe leg pain after a fracture','muscular','muscular',
 'Explain why swelling inside a closed fascial compartment can destroy muscle and nerve, and recognise compartment syndrome.',
 'A man has a cast applied for a broken tibia. Over the next hours, pain in the leg becomes severe and is worse when the toes are moved. The calf feels tense and his toes tingle.',
 ['tibialis-anterior','tibia','fibula','common-fibular-nerve'],
 'The leg is divided by the interosseous membrane, intermuscular septa and the tough deep fascia into anterior, lateral, superficial posterior and deep posterior compartments. Bleeding and swelling in a compartment cannot expand, so the pressure rises until it compresses the veins and then the arteries, cutting off muscle and nerve supply. The anterior compartment (tibialis anterior, extensor muscles, anterior tibial artery and deep fibular nerve) is the most often involved; early signs are pain out of proportion and pain on passive stretch of the toes, with numbness between the first and second toes (deep fibular nerve).',
 ['Suspect compartment syndrome in anyone with a limb injury and increasing pain that is out of proportion, especially after a cast or tight dressing.','Loosen or remove anything tight: bandages, casts (split the cast and padding down to the skin) and keep the limb at heart level, not raised.','Seek emergency help at once. Remember the signs: pain, tense swelling, pain on passive stretching, tingling; pallor, paralysis and absent pulses are late signs.','In hospital: measurement of compartment pressure when in doubt, and urgent fasciotomy (cutting open the fascia) to release the pressure.'],
 'Increasing pain after a limb injury, with a tight limb, is an emergency: delay can cause permanent loss of the limb.',
 [mc('Which nerve passes through the anterior compartment of the leg and is affected first in compartment syndrome?',['Deep fibular nerve','Tibial nerve','Sural nerve','Saphenous nerve'],0,'The deep fibular nerve supplies the anterior compartment muscles and the skin between the first and second toes.','intermediate','Name the nerve of the anterior leg compartment'),
  tf('Raising the limb well above the heart is advised if compartment syndrome is suspected.',False,'False. Keep the limb at heart level: raising it lowers the arterial pressure and worsens ischaemia.','advanced','Apply first aid for compartment syndrome')])
case('em-shoulder-dislocation','Dislocated shoulder','muscular','muscular',
 'Relate the anatomy of the shoulder to anterior dislocation and the risk to the axillary nerve.',
 'A footballer falls on his outstretched arm. His shoulder is flattened, he holds the arm away from his body, and there is numbness over the outer upper arm.',
 ['humerus','axillary-nerve','deltoid','scapula'],
 'The shallow glenoid cavity and the loose joint capsule allow great movement but also frequent dislocation, usually forwards and downwards, where the capsule is weakest. The axillary nerve winds round the surgical neck of the humerus and supplies deltoid and teres minor and the skin over the lower deltoid (the "regimental badge" area), so it can be stretched or injured. The axillary artery and brachial plexus lie close below the joint.',
 ['Support the arm in the position found, usually with a sling or a pillow. Do not try to put it back.','Apply a cold pack wrapped in cloth for 15-20 minutes at a time.','Check the pulse at the wrist, the colour and warmth of the hand, and feeling in the outer upper arm. Report numbness.','Go to the emergency department. A clinician reduces the dislocation after X-ray, and checks the axillary nerve and artery before and after.'],
 'A cold, pale or pulseless hand, or loss of feeling, needs emergency attention.',
 [mc('Which nerve is at risk in an anterior shoulder dislocation?',['Axillary nerve','Radial nerve at the elbow','Ulnar nerve at the elbow','Phrenic nerve'],0,'The axillary nerve lies against the surgical neck of the humerus and the inferior capsule.','foundation','Name the nerve at risk'),
  mc('Which muscle is supplied by the axillary nerve?',['Deltoid','Biceps brachii','Triceps brachii','Pectoralis major'],0,'The axillary nerve (C5, C6) supplies deltoid and teres minor.','foundation','Name the nerve supply of deltoid')])
# ---------------- skeletal
case('em-hip-fracture','Fall with a painful hip and a shortened leg','skeletal','appendicular',
 'Relate the blood supply of the femoral head to the complications of a fracture of the femoral neck.',
 'An 82-year-old woman falls at home. She cannot stand, and her right leg looks shorter and turned outwards.',
 ['femur','neck-of-femur','head-of-femur','hip-bone'],
 'The femoral head gets most of its blood from the medial and lateral circumflex femoral arteries, which send retinacular branches up the femoral neck, with a small contribution from the artery of the ligament of the head. A fracture of the neck can tear these vessels and cause avascular necrosis of the head. The pull of the muscles shortens the leg and rotates it outwards. The fracture can also bleed considerably into the thigh.',
 ['Call the emergency number. Do not move the person unless there is danger.','Keep the person still, and support the injured leg with padding (pillows, rolled blankets) in the position found. Do not try to straighten the leg.','Keep them warm and reassure them. Do not give food or drink in case surgery is needed.','Check for signs of shock (pale, cold, sweaty, fast pulse) and for any pain in the head, neck or back.','In hospital: pain relief, X-ray, and early surgery (usually within 24-36 hours) to fix the fracture or replace the hip.'],
 'Any suspected hip fracture needs an ambulance. Do not try to get the person to walk.',
 [mc('Which arteries supply the femoral head and neck?',['Medial and lateral circumflex femoral arteries','Obturator artery only','Superior gluteal artery','Popliteal artery'],0,'The circumflex femoral arteries (branches of the profunda femoris) give the main blood supply to the head and neck.','intermediate','Name the blood supply of the femoral head'),
  mc('What is a major complication of a displaced fracture of the femoral neck?',['Avascular necrosis of the femoral head','Frozen shoulder','Carpal tunnel syndrome','Spina bifida'],0,'Interruption of the retinacular vessels causes avascular necrosis of the head.','intermediate','State the complication')])
case('em-fracture-firstaid','Open fracture of the forearm','skeletal','appendicular',
 'Describe first aid for an open fracture and how to check the circulation and nerves of the limb.',
 'A girl falls off her bicycle. Her forearm looks deformed and a piece of bone sticks through the skin with bleeding.',
 ['radius','ulna','radial-artery','median-nerve'],
 'The radius and ulna are joined by an interosseous membrane. A break through the skin (an open fracture) lets germs into the bone and may cut vessels and nerves. The radial artery can be felt at the wrist, and the median nerve gives feeling to the thumb, index and middle fingers, so these are checked before and after splinting.',
 ['Call the emergency number if the fracture is open, severely deformed, bleeding heavily, or the hand is cold, pale or numb.','Control bleeding with firm pressure around (not on) any protruding bone, using a clean pad. Cover the wound with a clean dressing.','Do not push the bone back or try to straighten the limb. Immobilise the joints above and below the fracture with a splint (a rolled magazine or board) and a sling.','Check the pulse, colour, warmth and feeling in the hand before and after splinting.','Give nothing to eat or drink in case of surgery. In hospital: antibiotics, tetanus protection, and surgery to clean and fix the fracture.'],
 'Cold, pale or numb fingers, uncontrolled bleeding, or an open fracture need emergency care.',
 [mc('Which structure is checked at the wrist to assess the circulation to the hand?',['Radial pulse','Carotid pulse','Femoral pulse','Popliteal pulse'],0,'The radial artery can be felt at the wrist, lateral to the flexor carpi radialis tendon.','foundation','Check the circulation of the limb'),
  tf('A protruding bone end should be pushed back inside the wound.',False,'False. Do not push it back; cover it and splint the limb as found.','foundation','State first aid for an open fracture')])
case('em-flail-chest','Rib fractures and a chest wall that moves the wrong way','skeletal','axial',
 'Explain how multiple rib fractures can impair breathing and what first aid is appropriate.',
 'A driver is hurt in a crash. The left chest wall moves inwards when he breathes in and outwards when he breathes out, and he is in severe pain and short of breath.',
 ['ribs','sternum','pleura','left-lung'],
 'The ribs, costal cartilages and sternum form the rib cage, and the intercostal muscles and diaphragm expand it for breathing. If several adjacent ribs are broken in two places, a segment of chest wall (flail segment) is detached and moves paradoxically. The underlying lung is usually bruised, and broken rib ends can tear the pleura or lung (pneumothorax) or the intercostal vessels (haemothorax). Lower rib fractures can injure the spleen (left) or liver (right).',
 ['Call the emergency number.','Sit the person half upright in the position of greatest comfort. Support the injured side with their own hand or a folded towel or pillow held lightly.','Do not strap the chest tightly. Give oxygen if available and trained.','Watch breathing, colour and pulse, and be ready to start CPR if they collapse.','In hospital: pain relief (often a regional nerve block), oxygen, chest drain if there is air or blood in the pleural space, and help with ventilation if needed.'],
 'Severe pain with difficulty breathing after chest trauma is an emergency.',
 [mc('A flail chest usually needs how many broken ribs?',['Three or more adjacent ribs, each broken in two places','A single rib','Only the first rib','Only floating ribs'],0,'A flail segment needs at least three adjacent ribs each broken in two places.','intermediate','Define flail chest'),
  mc('Which organ can be injured by fractures of the lower left ribs?',['Spleen','Liver','Appendix','Urinary bladder'],0,'The spleen lies deep to the 9th-11th ribs on the left.','foundation','Relate rib fractures to underlying organs')])
# ---------------- lymphatic / immune
case('em-anaphylaxis','Allergic reaction with swelling and difficulty breathing','lymphatic','lymphatic',
 'Relate the immune response to the features of anaphylaxis and describe the use of an adrenaline auto-injector.',
 'A boy eats a biscuit containing nuts. Within minutes he develops hives, a swollen face and lips, wheeze, and becomes pale and floppy.',
 ['lymph-nodes','larynx','bronchial-tree','heart'],
 'Anaphylaxis is a severe allergic reaction in which immune cells (mast cells and basophils) release histamine and other mediators throughout the body. These cause widening and leakage of blood vessels (low blood pressure, swelling), narrowing of the bronchi (wheeze) and swelling of the larynx. Adrenaline narrows blood vessels, relaxes bronchial muscle and reduces swelling.',
 ['Use the adrenaline auto-injector at once if it is prescribed: remove the safety cap and press firmly into the outer middle of the thigh (it can be given through clothing). Hold for the time stated on the device (about 3 seconds for most models, 10 for some).','Call the emergency number and say "anaphylaxis". Note the time of the injection.','Lay the person flat with the legs raised. If they are struggling to breathe, let them sit up; a pregnant person lies on the left side; an unconscious person goes in the recovery position.','If there is no improvement after 5 minutes, give a second auto-injector if available.','Do not let the person stand or walk. They must go to hospital even if they seem better, as symptoms can come back. If they stop breathing normally, start CPR.'],
 'Swelling of the lips or tongue, wheeze, faintness, or collapse after a trigger is an emergency.',
 [mc('Into which site is an adrenaline auto-injector usually given?',['Outer middle part of the thigh','Upper arm only','Buttock','Abdomen'],0,'The outer thigh (vastus lateralis) gives rapid absorption and is easy to reach.','foundation','State the injection site'),
  mc('Which drug is first-line treatment for anaphylaxis?',['Adrenaline','Paracetamol','Antibiotics','Insulin'],0,'Intramuscular adrenaline is the first-line treatment.','foundation','Name the first-line drug')])
case('em-spleen-rupture','Left upper abdominal injury and shock','lymphatic','lymphatic',
 'Explain why the spleen is easily injured and why a splenic bleed causes left shoulder-tip pain and shock.',
 'A teenager is kicked in the left lower chest during a football match. Later he becomes pale, dizzy, with a fast pulse and pain in the left upper abdomen and the tip of the left shoulder.',
 ['spleen','ribs','diaphragm','phrenic-nerve'],
 'The spleen lies against the diaphragm under the 9th-11th left ribs. It is soft, very vascular (supplied by the splenic artery) and covered by a thin capsule, so blunt trauma can rupture it. Blood irritating the diaphragm causes shoulder-tip pain (Kehr sign) through the phrenic nerve (C3-C5). Blood loss causes shock.',
 ['Call the emergency number immediately.','Lay the person flat with the legs raised, and keep them warm. Do not give food or drink.','Watch breathing and pulse. Treat for shock and be ready to start CPR.','Do not press on the abdomen.','In hospital: fluids and blood, imaging (CT or ultrasound), and observation, embolisation or surgery.'],
 'Pain in the left upper abdomen after injury, with pallor or faintness, is an emergency.',
 [mc('Under which ribs does the spleen lie?',['Left 9th-11th ribs','Right 9th-11th ribs','Left 1st-3rd ribs','Right 5th-7th ribs'],0,'The spleen lies deep to the left 9th-11th ribs.','foundation','Locate the spleen'),
  mc('What is referred pain at the shoulder tip from diaphragmatic irritation called?',['Kehr sign','Murphy sign','Rovsing sign','Babinski sign'],0,'Kehr sign: left shoulder-tip pain from blood under the left diaphragm.','intermediate','Name the sign')])
case('em-sepsis','Infection with a high fever and confusion','lymphatic','lymphatic',
 'Describe how infection spreads along lymphatics and recognise the warning signs of sepsis.',
 'A man has a cut on his foot. Two days later the leg is red with a red streak running up the inner side, his groin glands are tender, and he now has a fever, a fast pulse and is confused.',
 ['lymph-nodes','superficial-popliteal-nodes','skin'],
 'Lymph drains from the foot through the lymphatics to the popliteal nodes and then to the inguinal nodes. Bacteria can follow this path (lymphangitis, with red streaks) and cause enlarged, tender nodes. When the infection spreads to the blood, the body\'s response can damage its own organs (sepsis), causing low blood pressure and confusion.',
 ['Seek emergency care if there is a fever or shivering, fast breathing or pulse, confusion or drowsiness, mottled skin, or little or no urine.','Do not wait to see if it improves. Early antibiotics and fluids save lives.','While waiting, keep the person warm and lying down. Do not give food or drink if they are drowsy.','Note the time symptoms began, the temperature and any medicines. In hospital: blood tests and cultures, intravenous antibiotics and fluids, and control of the source of infection.'],
 'Confusion, fast breathing, low blood pressure or mottled skin with infection is a medical emergency.',
 [mc('Which nodes first receive lymph from the foot and calf?',['Popliteal nodes, then inguinal nodes','Axillary nodes','Cervical nodes','Mesenteric nodes'],0,'Lymph from the foot and calf drains through the popliteal nodes (some goes directly to the inguinal nodes) to the deep inguinal nodes.','intermediate','Trace lymph drainage of the leg'),
  tf('Red streaks tracking up a limb from a wound suggest infection spreading in lymphatic vessels.',True,'True. This is lymphangitis.','foundation','Recognise lymphangitis')])
# ---------------- integumentary
case('em-burns','Scald burn','integumentary','integumentary',
 'Relate skin layers to the depth of a burn and describe first aid.',
 'A toddler pulls a cup of hot tea over her chest and arm. The skin is red, blistered and painful.',
 ['skin','epidermis','dermis','hypodermis'],
 'The skin has an epidermis, a dermis (with blood vessels, nerve endings, hair follicles and glands) and the hypodermis. A superficial burn damages only the epidermis (red, painful, no blisters). A partial-thickness burn extends into the dermis (blisters, very painful). In a full-thickness burn the dermis is destroyed, so the burn is white or charred and may be painless because nerve endings have been destroyed. Large burns cause fluid loss and shock, and burns around the neck can swell the airway.',
 ['Move away from the source of heat. Remove clothing and jewellery near the burn unless stuck to the skin.','Cool the burn with cool running tap water for 20 minutes (up to 3 hours after the injury still helps). Keep the rest of the person warm, especially children.','Do not use ice, butter, toothpaste or creams. Do not burst blisters.','Cover loosely with cling film (laid lengthwise, not wrapped round) or a clean non-fluffy dressing.','Call the emergency number or go to hospital for burns larger than the person\'s palm, burns of the face, hands, feet, genitals or over a joint, all full-thickness burns, chemical or electrical burns, and any burn in a child or older adult.'],
 'Burns with breathing difficulty, burns of the face or airway, or large areas need emergency care.',
 [mc('Which layer of skin contains the blood vessels and nerve endings?',['Dermis','Epidermis only','Stratum corneum','Hypodermis only'],0,'The dermis contains the capillaries, nerve endings, hair follicles and glands.','foundation','Name skin layers'),
  mc('How long should a burn be cooled under cool running water?',['20 minutes','10 seconds','1 minute','2 hours with ice'],0,'Cool running water for 20 minutes reduces damage; ice is not used.','foundation','State burn first aid')])
case('em-deep-cut','Deep cut on the hand','integumentary','integumentary',
 'Relate the skin, tendons, nerves and arteries of the hand to the assessment of a deep cut.',
 'A cook cuts the palm side of his index finger with a knife. It bleeds, and he cannot bend the tip of the finger and has no feeling on one side of the fingertip.',
 ['skin','dermis','median-nerve','radial-artery'],
 'The palmar skin of the finger is thin over the flexor tendons and the digital nerves and arteries, which run on each side of the finger. A deep cut can divide a tendon (loss of bending), a digital nerve (numb area on one side of the finger) or a digital artery (bleeding). The palmar skin is supplied by the median and ulnar nerves; the thumb, index, middle and half of the ring finger are supplied by the median nerve.',
 ['Control bleeding with firm direct pressure and elevate the hand above the heart.','Cover with a clean dressing. Do not clean deeply or probe the wound.','Check feeling and movement before bandaging: note any numbness, inability to bend the finger or paleness.','Seek medical care the same day, or emergency care if bleeding does not stop, or if the finger is pale, cold or cut off. Tendon and nerve injuries need early repair, and tetanus protection should be checked.'],
 'Uncontrolled bleeding, a white or cold finger, or a cut finger that cannot bend needs urgent assessment.',
 [mc('Which nerve supplies the skin of the palmar surface of the index finger?',['Median nerve','Radial nerve','Ulnar nerve','Axillary nerve'],0,'The median nerve (digital branches) supplies the palmar skin of the thumb, index, middle and radial half of the ring finger.','intermediate','Name the nerve supply of finger skin'),
  tf('Numbness on one side of a cut finger suggests injury to a digital nerve.',True,'True. A digital nerve runs on each side of the finger.','foundation','Recognise nerve injury')])
# ---------------- special senses
case('em-eye-chemical','Chemical splash in the eye','special-senses','somatic',
 'Relate the surface anatomy of the eye to the urgency of irrigation after a chemical splash.',
 'A worker splashes cleaning fluid into his eye. He has severe pain, the eye is red and watering, and he cannot open it.',
 ['eye','cornea','sclera','lacrimal-gland'],
 'The cornea and conjunctiva are exposed and very sensitive (nerve supply from the ophthalmic nerve, V1). Alkalis such as drain cleaners penetrate the cornea quickly, damaging the cells that regenerate the surface, and can cause blindness within minutes. The tear film from the lacrimal gland and the blink reflex help clear small amounts, but not chemicals.',
 ['Start irrigation immediately: hold the eye open and rinse with plenty of clean running water or sterile saline for at least 15-20 minutes. Do not wait for transport or for advice.','Tilt the head so that the water runs away from the other eye. Remove contact lenses if they come out easily.','Do not try to neutralise the chemical and do not rub the eye.','Take the chemical container (or the name) to the hospital. Go to the emergency department after irrigation: the eye must be checked.'],
 'Any chemical in the eye is an emergency: irrigate first, transport second.',
 [mc('What is the first step after a chemical splash in the eye?',['Rinse with running water for at least 15-20 minutes','Rub the eye','Apply a neutralising liquid','Cover with a pad and wait'],0,'Immediate, prolonged irrigation is the most important action.','foundation','State first aid for chemical eye injury'),
  mc('Which nerve gives sensation to the cornea?',['Ophthalmic nerve (V1)','Optic nerve (II)','Facial nerve (VII)','Maxillary nerve (V2)'],0,'The cornea is supplied by the long ciliary nerves from the nasociliary branch of the ophthalmic nerve (V1).','intermediate','Name the nerve supply of the cornea')])
case('em-retinal-artery','Sudden painless loss of vision in one eye','special-senses','somatic',
 'Relate the blood supply of the retina to central retinal artery occlusion.',
 'A 68-year-old man suddenly loses vision in his right eye. There is no pain, and the eye looks normal from the outside.',
 ['retina','central-retinal-artery','ophthalmic-artery','optic-disc'],
 'The ophthalmic artery, a branch of the internal carotid artery, gives off the central retinal artery, which enters the eye in the optic nerve and supplies the inner layers of the retina. It is an end artery. A clot or an embolus from a carotid plaque or the heart blocks it and the retina begins to die within about 90-100 minutes. The macula looks as a cherry-red spot against the pale retina on examination.',
 ['Treat sudden loss of vision as an emergency. Go straight to an eye emergency service or the emergency department, ideally within hours.','Note the time when the vision was lost and whether it returned. Do not wait for it to improve.','Do not drive. Do not take extra medicines unless advised.','In hospital: examination, urgent assessment of the carotid arteries, heart and blood vessels (this can be the warning of a stroke) and specialist treatment.'],
 'Sudden loss of vision, even if painless, is an emergency.',
 [mc('Which vessel supplies the inner layers of the retina?',['Central retinal artery','Short posterior ciliary arteries only','Lacrimal artery','Facial artery'],0,'The central retinal artery supplies the inner retina; the choroid (short posterior ciliary arteries) supplies the outer layers.','foundation','Name the blood supply of the retina'),
  mc('Which large artery gives rise to the ophthalmic artery?',['Internal carotid artery','External carotid artery','Vertebral artery','Subclavian artery'],0,'The ophthalmic artery arises from the internal carotid artery as it leaves the cavernous sinus.','foundation','Trace the origin of the ophthalmic artery')])
case('em-nosebleed','Nosebleed that will not stop','special-senses','somatic',
 'Relate the blood supply of the nasal septum to a nosebleed and describe first aid.',
 'A man has had a nosebleed for 20 minutes after sneezing. He takes aspirin for his heart.',
 ['nasal-cavity','nasal-cartilages','olfactory-epithelium'],
 'Most nosebleeds come from the anterior part of the nasal septum, Kiesselbach area (Little area), where branches of the anterior ethmoidal, sphenopalatine, greater palatine and superior labial arteries meet. The lining is thin and easily damaged by dryness, picking or sneezing. Bleeding from the posterior nose, from the sphenopalatine artery, is heavier and harder to control.',
 ['Sit upright and lean forward (not back), so blood does not run down the throat.','Pinch the soft part of the nose, just below the bony bridge, firmly for 10-15 minutes without letting go to check. Breathe through the mouth.','Spit out any blood. A cold pack on the bridge of the nose may help.','Do not blow the nose for several hours after the bleeding stops.','Seek emergency care if bleeding continues more than 20-30 minutes, follows a head injury, is heavy, or if the person takes blood thinners or feels faint.'],
 'Bleeding that does not stop after 20-30 minutes of proper pressure needs medical attention.',
 [mc('From which part of the nose do most nosebleeds arise?',['Anterior nasal septum (Kiesselbach area)','Olfactory epithelium','Posterior nasopharynx','Paranasal sinus'],0,'Most come from Kiesselbach area on the anterior septum.','foundation','Locate the source of most nosebleeds'),
  mc('How should a person with a nosebleed be positioned?',['Sitting and leaning forward','Lying flat with the head back','Standing with the head back','Lying on the back'],0,'Leaning forward prevents blood from running down the throat.','foundation','State the correct position')])
case('em-eye-penetrating','Object penetrating the eye','special-senses','somatic',
 'Explain why an object stuck in the eye must not be removed and describe protective first aid.',
 'A man hammering metal feels something strike his eye. A small metal fragment is visible in the eye and he has pain and blurred vision.',
 ['eye','sclera','cornea','lens'],
 'The cornea and sclera form the wall of the eye. A penetrating injury can pierce the wall, so that the aqueous or vitreous humour leaks, the iris or lens can be damaged, and infection can enter. Removing an object or pressing on the eye can make the damage worse because the object may be plugging the wound.',
 ['Do not try to remove the object, do not press on the eye and do not wash it.','Cover the eye loosely with a rigid shield (for example the bottom half of a paper cup taped over the bone around the eye) so that nothing touches the eye. Do not cover the other eye unless it is needed to reduce eye movement.','Keep the person calm and still, sitting upright. Do not give food or drink in case of surgery.','Go to an emergency department or call the emergency number. Wear goggles in future for similar work.'],
 'Any injury that may have penetrated the eye needs emergency eye care.',
 [mc('What should be done with an object stuck in the eye?',['Leave it and protect the eye with a rigid shield','Pull it out quickly','Rinse the eye vigorously','Press a pad firmly on the eye'],0,'Do not remove the object; protect the eye with a rigid shield and get urgent care.','foundation','State first aid for penetrating eye injury'),
  mc('Which layers form the wall of the eyeball at the front and back?',['Cornea at the front and sclera behind','Retina and lens','Iris and ciliary body','Choroid only'],0,'The cornea at the front and the sclera at the back and sides form the outer fibrous layer.','foundation','Name the outer layer of the eye')])
